WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.780
The results are in and the U.S. intelligence
community, the Mueller Report, and the Senate Intelligence Committee all agree.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:11.580
Russia interfered with the 2016 U.S. presidential
election.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:16.820
And there’s good evidence that Russian President
Vladimir Putin personally ordered these attacks.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:21.860
The goal was to weaken Hillary Clinton’s
campaign and undermine the U.S. electoral process.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:25.980
According to the Mueller Report, the government of Russia perceived it would benefit from

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:29.500
a Donald Trump presidency and worked to secure that outcome.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:34.020
Of course, there is still sharp partisan debate
about how much this Russian meddling affected

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:39.440
the final outcome of the 2016 race because nobody really knows for sure.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:46.280
There is also partisan debate about what steps the U.S. must take to prevent this sort of thing from happening again.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.940
But first, let’s go back to 2016.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:56.880
Russian state-backed media published false
stories about Clinton.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:02.660
Then hundreds of Russian backed trolls, often posing as Americans, posted those stories on social media.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:07.400
On Facebook alone, those posts reached over 140 million people.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:13.260
And Russian hackers leaked emails from the Democratic National Committee and Clinton campaign chair John Podesta.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.000
But it didn’t stop there.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.120
Moscow also targeted election systems in all
50 states.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.500
They went after voter data and the machines
that tally and store votes.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:27.460
Russian hackers came close to changing or
deleting votes in at least one state.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:33.200
And while there’s no evidence they altered
voter information, it shows how vulnerable our elections truly are.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.860
2016 wasn't an isolated incident.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:41.000
In the 2018 midterm elections, Russian trolls
spread disinformation and stirred up divisions

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.840
on issues like abortion and gun control.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:47.620
Intelligence officials believe they will interfere again in 2020.

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:50.520
In fact, it's probably already underway.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:52.940
And it’s not just happening in the United
States.

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:59.180
There is strong evidence that Russia has tried to meddle in the affairs of at least 27 other countries since 2004.

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:03.700
Canada, the United Kingdom, Ukraine, and Germany have all been targeted.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:06.960
So how can the U.S. keep its elections safe from foreign interference?

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.320
For starters, the U.S. can try to deter Russian
cyber aggression.

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:16.260
This means sanctioning Russian officials and
showing that the U.S. can launch counter-attacks.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:20.680
Washington can make voting systems more difficult
to hack in the first place.

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:27.080
Election officials can back up machines with paper ballots and use other safeguards in case those hackers do break in.

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:32.540
Congress can also start to regulate social
media sites to stem the flood of misinformation.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:37.900
Social media companies have made some moves
on their own, like hiring fact-checkers to review posts.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:44.460
But many say these companies need to be forced
to do more, like disclose who’s paying for political ads on their sites.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:47.500
Since 2016, some things have been done.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:52.520
Both the Obama and Trump administrations imposed
sanctions on Russian individuals and entities.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:56.400
Under President Obama, 35 Russian diplomats
were expelled.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:03.100
The FBI and other intelligence agencies,
they are spending more time and resources on investigating electoral foul play.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:08.440
Congress has allocated $380 million dollars
to states to better protect the vote.

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:15.540
Many states are now investing in new machines, better software, and preparing for post-election audits for 2020.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:20.460
Unfortunately, experts say it will take billions
to truly secure elections.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:25.080
And electoral security is now becoming a 
divisive political issue.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:28.960
President Trump has referred to reports of
meddling as a “hoax.”

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:32.440
And he has dismissed warnings about
the 2020 elections.

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:38.820
Senate Majority Leader Mitch McConnell has also accused Democrats of playing up Russian interference for political gain.

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:42.020
As a result, greater voter protections have
stalled.

00:03:42.029 --> 00:03:46.200
And American votes remain vulnerable to future
attacks.

